  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/... \nLate in the week, shortwave energy will have ejected northeast and \nbecome absorbed into the stronger westerly flow over the plains. In \nits wake, deep quasi-zonal westerly flow will gradually scour away \nmoisture such that PWATS fall below 1\" and boundary layer mixing \nratios under 9 g/kg by Friday afternoon. A period of shortwave \nridging through the first half of the weekend will also impose \nexpansive synoptic scale subsidence such that only the most \nsusceptible high terrain locations would support brief afternoon \ndeep convection. In fact, instability and ascent parameters are so \npoor Saturday that NBM POPs are essentially under 5% across the \nentire region. \n\nForecast confidence deteriorates significantly next week due \nuncertainties in a progressive westerly wave and potential \ninteraction with tropical moisture. Ensemble members are in good \nagreement that a shortwave will deepen near the California coast \nearly next week, then rapidly propagate through the intermountain \nWest during the first half of the week. However, there is \nsubstantial differences in depth and timing of this feature, which \nwill be crucial in determining the ability to tap deep, tropical \nmoisture and resulting impacts across the SW Conus. In general, \noperational members are deeper and faster with this wave than the \npreponderance of NAEFS membership; and based on historical \nprecedent, would favor a somewhat slower progression of the central \ncold core and trough axis though intensity towards the deeper \nsolutions is preferred. While conceptually a slower, deeper outcome \nwould favor drawing the full brunt of remnant tropical circulation \nand associated moisture into the Southwest (akin to the latest 12Z \nGFS), the wide uncertainty in the actual location of the tropical \nsystem combined with disparity in the location and timing of the \ntrough passage yields a large variation of possible outcomes. \nNevertheless at the very least, ensembles hint at jet energy and \nsheared shortwave drawing midlevel moisture inland Sunday/Monday \nyielding scattered showers and storms. Thereafter because of the \nvast ensemble spread, mandated NBM POPs are essentially modest \nvalues spread over a prolonged period and offer minimal actionable \ndetails at this time. \n\nIn term of temperatures, much like during the short term, not much \nfluctuation in day-to-day readings is expected to occur. Daily \nvalues will hover right around to just above normal, which \ntranslates to lower desert readings ranging between 94-105 degrees \nthrough the weekend. Overnight lows will be slightly more elevated \ncompared to where they typically are for late September, likely an \neffect of lingering moisture and cloud cover (at least for some \nspots), with readings between 70-82 degrees. Meanwhile higher \nelevation conditions will be quite comfortable as most locations \nshould be in the 80s, with perhaps a few spots touching the lower \n90s. Lows for these spots in the upper 50s to lower 70s will be \ncommon.",
